From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter Laux <hunterlaux@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 17:06:17 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 08/74] wifi: rtw88: usb: Don't return stale data from a failed
 register read

rtw_usb_read() reads into one of a ring of buffers and returns the
buffer's contents whatever the transfer did. When the control transfer
fails or comes back short, that is all or part of the value an earlier
read left there, returned as if it had just been read from the chip.

Clear the buffer before the transfer, so a failed read returns 0 and a
short one zeros in the bytes it didn't get, and report short reads as
well as failed ones.
---
 drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/usb.c | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/usb.c b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/usb.c
index 64e1c3420..0b35b481a 100644
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/usb.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/realtek/rtw88/usb.c
@@ -86,11 +86,13 @@ static u32 rtw_usb_read(struct rtw_dev *rtwdev, u32 addr, u16 len)
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&rtwusb->usb_lock, flags);
 
 	data = &rtwusb->usb_data[idx];
+	/* What a failed or short read leaves: not an earlier read's value */
+	*data = 0;
 
 	ret = usb_control_msg(udev, usb_rcvctrlpipe(udev, 0),
 			      RTW_USB_CMD_REQ, RTW_USB_CMD_READ, addr,
 			      RTW_USB_VENQT_CMD_IDX, data, len, 1000);
-	if (ret < 0 && ret != -ENODEV && count++ < 4)
+	if (ret != len && ret != -ENODEV && count++ < 4)
 		rtw_err(rtwdev, "read register 0x%x failed with %d\n",
 			addr, ret);
 
